Dear Craig & list,

I've had the same kind of thing happen, same messages and everything.  The 
problem was an empty /boot/etc/aboot.conf file.  I fixed it by putting in the 
appropriate lines ... for instance in my machine it was:


 #aboot default configurations
# NOTICE: You have a /boot partition. This means that
#         all kernel paths are relative to /boot/
0:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32 root=/dev/sdc3 initrd=/initrd-2.4.9-32.img
 console=tty0
1:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32 root=/dev/sdc3 initrd=/initrd-2.4.9-32.img
 console=ttyS0
2:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32 root=/dev/sdc3 initrd=/initrd-2.4.9-32.img
 console=ttyS0 console=tty0
3:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32 root=/dev/sdc3 initrd=/initrd-2.4.9-32.img  single

The numbers are the boot options aboot puts on you if you don't boot with a 
flag.

I used swriteboot a time or twqo, it never even touched the aboot.conf file.

Well that's my 2 cents.

jon norstog


