On Tue, Mar 04, 2003 at 12:55:01PM +0300, AlexCH wrote:

Don't respond "jeopardy style".  Makes longer discussions very
unreadable.

> It seems, your AHA-1742 controller does not supported by the kernel
> provided by RedHat CDs.

If that would be the case then the original poster would not install
anything at all.  None of SCSI controllers is "supported" by these
kernels if you are not loading initrd.

> RCM>   Trouble is I can not boot from the hard disk(s) after completing the install.
.....
> 
> RCM>         After installation is complete, perform a reboot (power down) and enter the following at the SRM prompt:     b dkb1 -fi vmlinuz -fl root=/dev/sdb1

You will fare better if you will read instructions and boot with

   b dkb1 -fl 0

Installer writes a correct initial aboot.conf.  This assumes that 'dkb1'
is indeed your boot device but a boot command you are trying to use is
clearly wrong.  Error messages you are seeing are normal and expected
with what you typed.

There is a catch on Alpha.  Scripts in kernel update rpms do NOT, at
least as for now, rewrite 'aboot.conf' to reflect version changes.  If
you will forget about that and skip an editing step then, after a kernel
update which removed old boot images and corresponding initrds, you have
to type, at least once, a correct boot command manualy.  Assuming that
you set properly 'bootdef_dev' in your SRM then an easy way is to boot
with

  b -fl i

You will get into 'aboot> ' prompt where you can list names of files
on your disk and also a content of aboot.conf.  Makes easier to type
a modified boot command when you are seeing full correct syntax.


