To the list:

I was thinking about getting a slightly hotter system than my present 
pws500a/u and have been scouting eBay ...  

this last week a late model pws500a with lots of ram, a couple hard drives 
and extras went for about $325 ...

A ratted-out XP1000/500 with no RAM or video card went for about $750.   And 
a 667mhz cpu & board with a little RAM got picked up by a well-known retailer 
of Alpha hardware for over $1,000.

It looks to me like the "Miata" class machines are the best overall value at 
this time, and that demand may actually be increasing for late-model Alphas.  
Perhaps this is temporary, and prices will fall when more EV6x machines start 
coming off-lease.  Or perhaps a lot of sys-admins are buying relatively cheap 
Alphas to expand existing Tru64 systems  ... since Itanium is, shall we say, 
not quite ready for prime time?

There was a recent discussion on the Tru64.org system administrators' list, 
where the consensus was, no one wants to be an early adopter, the 
applications aren't there yet, and the main options are:  expand existing 
EV6x/Tru64 systems or upgrade to EV7.  
just a thought

jn




