On Feb. 21, my UP2000 motherboard died.  It was my only machine,
so I went and bought a "temporary" X86 setup and installed RH8.0
on it.  It's a Pentium 4 "A" with 512KB cache at 1.8GHz.  The
mobo is an Iwill P4ES with 512MB of ECC DDR 266MHz, alias PC2100.
(Evidently, they use cheap DIMM sockets, because it took several
attempts at memory insertion before the BIOS would come up.  They
also don't do very good compatibility testing on the motherboard,
because it would behave differently depending on which type of
graphics card was installed.)

The surprise to me was that this thing is actually much faster on
all of my java code than the Alpha.  The Alpha machine in
question was/is a 667MHz EV67 (one CPU) on a UP2000 mobo, using
Compaq's Java and RH7.2 (upgraded a week before it died).  I'm
seeing speed ratios between 2 and 6, always in the X86 machine's
favor.

The dark side (X86) machine in question is using Sun's 1.4.1 J2
SDK.  The Alpha machine was running Compaq's 1.3.1 Java package,
but not using the "fast JVM".  Compaq's Java compiled to native
code, so I would expect it to be pretty fast (once it did the
compilation).  One of the programs in question takes 3 minutes on
the Alpha, 30 seconds on the dark side (X86) machine, so compile
time should (hopefully) be negligible.

So, what I'm wondering is whether there is some reason Sun's JVM
is massively more efficient than Compaq's, or whether the (cheap,
little) architecturally nasty X86 chip is that much faster than
an Alpha.

Any ideas?

>From an architectural standpoint, I really like the Alpha and
despise the X86 (even though I oversaw the schematic formal
verification efforts on the Pentium Pro and first Pentium 4).
However, if Alpha is that much behind in performance already, I
may not be able to resist the pull of the architectural dark
side, especially considering the difference in cost (the X86 mobo
and CPU were only $250 together).

Thanks.

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net




