On Tue, 11 Mar 2003, Michael Huntingdon wrote:

>Seems to me that HP just released Marvel, based on the EV7 chipset.

Yes, they have.

>And...oh my, but haven't I also heard something about EV79.

That's in the pipe as well it seems, yes.

>And...if Itanium II is late..

Not sure what you mean by that.  Itanium II has been out for 
quite a while now.

>would we see EV8?

That has been answered by HP already.

>Probably not but I've been accused of being overly optimistic in
>the past.

I've also been accused of the same with respect to Alpha.

>Alpha isn't dead.

That truely depends on the definition of "dead" which you choose
to use.

Hypothetically speaking, if you define "dead" as "there are zero
Alpha processors in existance anymore", or "there are no
computers still in operation which are powered by Alpha
processors", or even "there is zero interest by neither 
businesses nor individuals in the alpha processor" - as being 
your definition of "dead", then you're correct.  Alpha is not 
dead, as there are numerous people out there (myself included) 
who consider the Alpha to be a wonderful CPU architecture, and 
would like to see it "live on" as long as possible one way or 
another.

However... Compaq decommissioned Alpha prior to the merger with
Hewlett Packard.  If you read the press releases from Compaq, as
well as those of Hewlett Packard, they very clearly state that
the Alpha architecture is more or less in a maintenance mode.  
In other words - Alpha is dead.  You'll have to go find their 
specific press releases for their own wordings however.

As much as I would love to see EV8 come to exist, and as much as
I would like to see Alpha architecture remain in production, and
be improved, etc. - that is very unlikely to happen.  There are
processor roadmaps on the web, and how long the processors will
be supported.


>Perhaps in two or three or four years we will all look around at
>how much progress has been made in the sciences, consider how
>our lives have been enriched by it, and it just may occur to
>some that "Wow..Alpha wasn't dead in 2001, 2002, or 2003" as so
>many claim.

It depends only on how pedantic you want to be.  I'll be using 
Alpha until the last processor blows it's last transistor, 
however there is a big huge difference between "alpha exists 
still" and "alpha is alive and evolving into the future".  Alpha 
does "exist", yes, but it is far from not-dead.

Either way, it is only playing with words.

-- 
