On Wednesday, March 12, 2003, at 10:49  AM, Mike A. Harris wrote:
> On Tue, 11 Mar 2003, Michael Huntingdon wrote:
>> And...if Itanium II is late..
>
> Not sure what you mean by that.  Itanium II has been out for
> quite a while now.
>> Alpha isn't dead.
>
> That truely depends on the definition of "dead" which you choose
> to use.

Itanium is as "out" as Alpha is "dead."

HP is committed to selling NEW Alphas, including EV7/9 well into the 
future. Not only do do they constantly restate that fact, but there are 
contractual documents out supporting the ability to purchase new Alpha 
designs well into 2005.

If you consider that dead, then I'll consider Itanium as out.

As far as Itanium is concerned, everybody knows that what is Itanium 
today won't be Itanium next year. Meaning that there won't be any kind 
of upward compatibility between Itanium II and IA64 due in about 
another 18 months. It's not going to be the simple swap an x86 for an 
x+86 deal that Intel has offered in the past. And the costs are now 
rumored to be an order of magnitude greater per chip. Although the 
performance gain over the existing Itanium II may be as equally great. 
But that won't be till late 2004 or mid 2005.

Till then we'll have to wonder what the Power 640 is going to do this 
summer.

T.T.F.N.
