On Wed, 12 Mar 2003, William H. Magill wrote:

>> Not sure what you mean by that.  Itanium II has been out for
>> quite a while now.
>>> Alpha isn't dead.
>>
>> That truely depends on the definition of "dead" which you choose
>> to use.
>
>Itanium is as "out" as Alpha is "dead."

My only point really was that:  If you go based on published
press release announcements of Compaq and now HP, HP and Intel
are pushing Itanium technology going forward, and Alpha is
considered to be in a maintenance mode.  The Alpha technology was
sold to Intel, and it is at least rumored that many of the Alpha
engineers went to Intel and/or AMD, although I haven't read any 
official information to that effect.  Just word through the 
grapevine.


>HP is committed to selling NEW Alphas, including EV7/9 well into
>the future. Not only do do they constantly restate that fact,
>but there are contractual documents out supporting the ability
>to purchase new Alpha designs well into 2005.

Absolutely.

>If you consider that dead, then I'll consider Itanium as out.

Again, as I stated, it depends greatly on every single person's 
specific definition of "dead".  The market as a whole considers 
Alpha "dead" because HP/Compaq have declared the processor to 
being phased out in favour of Itanium technology.  That doesn't 
mean it was turned off like a lightbulb and is no longer 
available and no longer being produced.  It means that it is not 
being actively researched and developed indefinitely, and that 
the product's lifespan has been determined and published via 
press releases.  I can go and hunt down those off the web if it's 
necessary to prove a point, but I figured everyone was aware of 
that horrible news years ago when it was published.

I'm well aware of Alpha EV7 and EV79, and I'm personally a major
Alpha fan.  I would love nothing more than to see EV8, EV9, EV10,
etc. and to have a 8Ghz Alpha on my desktop in 6-8 years.


>As far as Itanium is concerned, everybody knows that what is Itanium 
>today won't be Itanium next year.

Well, I've got one of those on my desk too (Itanium II).  Please, 
do tell me more.  ;o)


>Meaning that there won't be any kind of upward compatibility
>between Itanium II and IA64 due in about another 18 months.

I'm not sure what you mean by "upward compatibility".  I must 
confess to not knowing a lot about the existing IA64 technology 
nor future IA64 technology plans however (I'm an Alpha fan 
remember)  <grin>

"Backward compatibility" in a new processor would mean "new
processor is capable of running software written for old
processor in family".  I'm not sure what is meant by "upward
compatibility" other than to guess it means the opposite of 
"backward compatibility" in which case I wouldn't be surprised as 
old processors generally aren't capable of running software 
written specifically for new processors which didn't exist when 
the old processor was produced.  ;o)

>It's not going to be the simple swap an x86 for an x+86 deal
>that Intel has offered in the past. And the costs are now
>rumored to be an order of magnitude greater per chip. Although
>the performance gain over the existing Itanium II may be as
>equally great.  But that won't be till late 2004 or mid 2005.

Any pointers to this info?  (assuming it is public)


>Till then we'll have to wonder what the Power 640 is going to do this 
>summer.

Personally, I believe Alpha technology to be superior to any 
other processor architecture developed by anyone to date, so I'm 
upset to see Alpha get killed off.  Who knows, perhaps a miracle 
will revive it now.  ;o)

Assuming that doesn't happen though, I believe all things 
considered, that AMD x86_64 technology and IBM PPC64 technology 
will be the leading CPU architectures over the next 5 years at 
least.

Take care,
TTYL

P.S. Wouldn't it be nice to see EV8 silicon?

P.S.S. Please note that the opinions and comments shared in this
entire message above are those of my own personally and not that
of Red Hat Inc. or anyone else.  In other words, spoken as a
computer geek with my fedora off.  ;o)

-- 
