On Wednesday, March 12, 2003, at 03:34  PM, Mike A. Harris wrote:
>> It's not going to be the simple swap an x86 for an x+86 deal
>> that Intel has offered in the past. And the costs are now
>> rumored to be an order of magnitude greater per chip. Although
>> the performance gain over the existing Itanium II may be as
>> equally great.  But that won't be till late 2004 or mid 2005.
>
> Any pointers to this info?  (assuming it is public)

I am not aware of any statements by Intel. However, HP has provided 
"roadmaps" as part of the Alpha migration which has plotted IA64 as 
2004 availability.

However, since I got laid off by the University in September, the last 
roadmap I saw was Spring's. Since then the word is that it has slipped 
into 2005... with tape-out in late 2004.

What I'm talking about is Itanium with "Alpha inside." The Alpha 
engineering team did move, and has remained virtually intact, to Intel 
as part of the original "sale." That is part of the original joint 
press announcement from Intel/HP.

The last stories I heard over the summer had the new IA64 as being 
based on the EV8 technology and consequently a very big re-design. The 
primary reason was that the existing Itanium II design was simply not 
functioning anywhere near projected speeds and consuming something like 
80 watts per chip... much too hot.
What I have not been able to get a solid answer on is -- EPIC was 
developed because neither Intel nor HP had the compiler technology to 
allow "optimization" to occur at the compiler level. Digital and IBM 
hold all of the necessary patents in that area, and they didn't want to 
deal... at the time. As part of the Intel deal, and largely 
"unobserved" was the transfer of the compiler technology ALSO to Intel. 
Intel began releasing new "Digital based" compilers almost immediately 
after the ink was dry. The first SDK was released before the HPQ deal 
was voted on. That SDK apparently gave enough of a performance change 
to the Itanium that Intel was "convinced" of adopting the Alpha design 
teams approach to things over their own version. Consequently the 2004 
IA64 is a radical departure from today's Itanium II.

Interestingly, last I heard, the "IA64 product" has been removed from 
the jurisdiction of ISS - the "Industry Standard Systems" - group, and 
given to the Enterprise Systems Group. Both because of Intel's stated 
"goal" that the Chip will not be available for use in "desktop" system 
but only in Servers. The main reason I've heard is the simple fact that 
the IA64 chips are going to be dramatically more expensive than the x86 
family for the projected future.

So, right now, it appears that the IA64 is going to be far more Alpha 
like than x86 like. This implies that board design and subsequent 
system design is also going to be much different -- apparently even 
when compared to some of the existing Itanium implementations.

Alas, finding work in the HPQ Enterprise Level world is "tough" to put 
it mildly - Everybody wants Solaris, except for the two people who want 
AIX. So, consequently, it's looking more and more like I'll be watching 
strictly from the sidelines. I've been doing more Mac OS X consulting 
than anything lately. Hopefully, things will change with the Spring 
thaw.

T.T.F.N.
