On Wednesday, March 12, 2003, at 07:29  PM, rm.riches@verizon.net wrote:
> Interesting information and analysis.  Could you please
> clarify, if you know, at what level is "the new IA64
> ... based on the EV8 technology":
>
>   Instruction set architecture level: This would mean they
>   are scrapping the EPIC instruction set and using the Alpha
>   instruction set, just calling it IA64.  This would imply
>   incompatibility at the binary executable level between the
>   old IA64 and the new one.  It might or might not imply
>   compatibility between Alpha and the new IA64.
>
>   Microarchitecture level: This would mean they are keeping
>   the EPIC instruction set but designing the chip's internal
>   structures more closely to the Alpha chips than the
>   existing Itanic chips.  This would imply binary executable
>   compatibility between the old IA64 and the new one.
>
>   Logic and circuit design level: This would mean they are
>   using clock distribution, clock gating, pipe stage
>   latching, power control, combinational logic style,
>   internal memory array style, etc. techniques more similar
>   to the Alpha chips than the existing Itanic chips.  This
>   would also imply binary executable compatibility between
>   the old IA64 and the new one.
>
> As a former chip designer (17 years experience), the
> difference between those three levels is significant.

You're way ahead of me there...  I'm on the software side of the house.
However, I've gotten the impression that 2 and 3 (top to bottom) are 
where the Alpha design folks have made their mark. However, I don't 
know that it has been an either-or environment. I've gotten the 
impression that the N-way-SMP on a chip stuff is sufficiently different 
from either pre-existing design that it didn't much matter. One rumor I 
heard was that the end design was quite "soft" in that it can provide 
several levels(?) of instruction emulation... much the way the Vax had 
PDP instructions for a couple of revs and the AMD chip does both 32 and 
64 bit instructions. The original Itanium design from years ago had the 
"lock step" instructions needed by Tandem, and so did the EV8. But I 
don't know if "binary compatibility" is a design criteria as the 
"binary emulation" technology from Digital (vax on alpha) was also part 
of the Alpha deal.

I was thinking more of compatibility on the packaging issues of memory 
and I/O buss interfaces - NUMA related stuff. Compaq/Alpha had the 
licenses for them which Intel did not. (From Sequent, I think it was, 
but I forget.) From what I have been able to guess, a lot of this "next 
generation" hardware is going back to Cray's original issues of "long 
wires take nano-seconds to navigate." Density does become an important 
issue at the backplane level.

I've also heard that Alpha-IA64 runs significantly cooler than existing 
Itaniums.

I've heard mixed stories about the EPIC instruction set versa Alpha's, 
but they all revolve around the importance of compiler level 
optimization capability which HP and Intel did not have access to 
before, so I don't know what that really means. (And, again, none of my 
information is current.)

Because all of the IA64 circa 2004 stuff is aimed at the seriously 
high-end market, I suspect that binary compatibility is not going to be 
that big an issue. That market tends not to be as dependent upon 
"non-portable" and "non-recompileable" code. At least from HPQ's point 
of view, all of the Vax to Alpha and everybody else to Alpha porting 
tools are available. Both Tru64 and VMS were running on IA64 in 2001, 
so the implication is that they can be ported to whatever instruction 
set the compilers put out "pretty easily." And I got the impression 
that the experience so garnered was going to be used to get the Q ISVs 
ported in record time. Since, nominally, all of their apps were already 
64 bit clean. I don't know the impact/disposition of the fact that the 
original Itanium was capable of switching Endian modes, but that was 
one of the biggest issue and difference between Tru64 and HP-UX. So the 
idea of taking the VMS locking code which had been "ported" to Tru64 
and "converting" it for HP-UX didn't seem to bother folks. The idea was 
that neither of the existing Unix operating system would run on IA64, 
but rather one that would "look like" HP-UX with Tru64 clustering, 
AdvFS and other stuff imbedded in it. I don't know if VMS is still a 
live operating system for IA64 or is as dead as alpha at this point... 
it seems to have more lives than a cat.

One read of the comment by Intel that IA64 would not run on the desktop 
could be that it is because it is NOT binary compatible. Just a wild 
guess.

But don't forget, I'm about 9 months behind on the rumor mill at this 
time.

Here's a question I don't have the answer to -- is anybody BUT HPQ 
expected to be a volume purchaser of IA64? I can't think of anyone else 
in that league these days, especially if the per chip cost is as high 
as it seems it is going to be.
That alone will knock Dell out of the running. But that is a viscous 
circle. Alpha chips cost so much because the made so few of them 
because nobody was using them because they cost so much.

All I can say for certain is that, so far at least, it appears that 
Digital Stealth Marketing is alive and well at HPQ.


