> tools are available. Both Tru64 and VMS were running on IA64 in 2001,
> so the implication is that they can be ported to whatever instruction
> set the compilers put out "pretty easily." And I got the impression
> that the experience so garnered was going to be used to get the Q ISVs
> ported in record time. Since, nominally, all of their apps were
> already 64 bit clean. I don't know the impact/disposition of the fact
> that the original Itanium was capable of switching Endian modes, but
> that was one of the biggest issue and difference between Tru64 and
> HP-UX. So the idea of taking the VMS locking code which had been
> "ported" to Tru64 and "converting" it for HP-UX didn't seem to bother
> folks. The idea was that neither of the existing Unix operating
> system would run on IA64, but rather one that would "look like" HP-UX
> with Tru64 clustering, AdvFS and other stuff imbedded in it. I don't
> know if VMS is still a live operating system for IA64 or is as dead
> as alpha at this point... it seems to have more lives than a cat.
> 
> One read of the comment by Intel that IA64 would not run on the
> desktop could be that it is because it is NOT binary compatible. Just
> a wild guess.
> 
> But don't forget, I'm about 9 months behind on the rumor mill at this
> time.
> 
> Here's a question I don't have the answer to -- is anybody BUT HPQ
> expected to be a volume purchaser of IA64? I can't think of anyone
> else in that league these days, especially if the per chip cost is as
> high as it seems it is going to be.
> That alone will knock Dell out of the running. But that is a viscous
> circle. Alpha chips cost so much because the made so few of them
> because nobody was using them because they cost so much.
> 
> All I can say for certain is that, so far at least, it appears that
> Digital Stealth Marketing is alive and well at HPQ.
> 
> 
> T.T.F.N.
