To the list:

I picked up an 8 meg Permedia2 video card and some 68-pin SCSI CDroms  from 
an eBay seller this week.  The card is a Diamond Fire GL 1000, it looks like 
it was pulled from a system.

I am running it now at 1200x1024, true color on RedHat 7.2  It worked pretty 
well on Windows 2000 as well.  The driver for this card was included in the 
RC2 distribution ... haven't tried it on NT yet.  On Tru64 (5.1a), it needs 
the comet driver.  I tried it but the OS didn't autodetect (Tru64 does that, 
sometimes) and it ran VGA.  I found the comet driver module and plan to load 
it into the Tru64 kernel when I have time. Then I'll be able to triple-boot 
into a decent screen without swapping video cards.

The card was $9.95

I had a lot of trouble with the IDE CDrom drive on my PWS500a.  A while ago I 
bought a 68-pin IBM SCSI CD drive off the internet.  IT was a 20X made by 
PLextor, and seems to be SCSI 2 wide - but may be ultra, hard tell .  I've 
had no more problems reading CDs since i changed over, and it fits right up 
to the 68-pin ribbon cable on my PWS.  It only has 3 scsi ID jumpers, but it 
does have a standard sound cable lead.

I just bought 5 of these drives for $9.95 total.  

I got a couple other items on auctions, the guy running them boxed everything 
up in one shipment and sent it out for $15.00.

It looks like they have plenty of the drives and the cards left.

The place selling this stuff is 

pcsurplusonline.com

and the guy who runs the auctions is 
  
"Tim" <pcpisales@pcpartsinc.com>

He might be willing to sell direct without going through the auction process.


I don't work for this company or anything, but i've seen a lot of postings 
about video cards and not being able to read CDs.

jn





