On Tue, Mar 04, 2003 at 07:05:39PM +1100, Ryder, Craig M. wrote:
>
> 	Am (still) trying to get Redhat 7.2 running on a DEC ALPHA
> 2100 (A500MP) machine.  Am now using an ADPTEC 1742A (EISA slot 3)as
> the SCSI controller for the hard disks.  I boot from the installed
> CDROM drive, attached to a NCR53C810 (EISA slot1), using the command
> 'b dka6 -fl 3' for text based installation.  The installation
> procedure runs through to completion with no errors.  Trouble is I
> can not boot from the hard disk(s) after completing the install.
> 
> 	During the installation I select the autopartioning function
> and the following mount points are assigned:
> 	/dev/sda1	/boot
> 	/dev/sdb1	/
> 	/dev/sdc1	/var
> 	/dev/sdd1	/home
> 	/dev/sde1	/usr
> 	/dev/sdf1	swap
> 
> Note:   Each scsi disk is a separate physical unit (2.1 gig ea)
> 
> 	After installation is complete, perform a reboot (power down)
> and enter the following at the SRM prompt: b dkb1 -fi vmlinuz -fl
> root=/dev/sdb1
> 
> 	The hard disk (sda) access light illuminate and Linux starts
> to load and then fails with the following messages:
> 
> 	cannot open root device on sdb1 or 08:21, please append a
> correct 'root' boot option.  kernel panic: VFS: unable to mount root
> file system on 08:21.

Do you get messages during the boot process that show the SCSI disks
being found by the Adaptec driver? If not, then somehow your INITRD
was left without the appropriate module being included.

Boot into the "rescue" mode from the CD, and look at /etc/modules.conf,
I think it should have a "scsi_hostadapter" line that indicates the
Adaptec driver, perhaps "aha1740"? If it's not there, you could put one
there, then re-run "mkinitrd". Note that you might have to give some
additional flags for that EISA Adaptec, such as IRQ and address, which
you may be able to get from doing an "lspci -v" during "rescue" mode.

Good luck.

 --Jay++

