On Tue, 18 Mar 2003, Alan Young wrote:

> 
> GCC 3.x changed the application binary interface (ABI) of the C++
> symbols.  It is not compatible with GCC 2.x ABI format.
> Mozilla does it's own resolving of the C++ function labels in the
> shared libraries that it loads.  This is in the xpcom logic in
> mozilla/xpcom/reflect/xptcall directories.  Stock mozilla only 
> supports GCC 2.x.  The assembler code in the xptcall directories
> are setup for that.  When a GCC 3.x compiled mozilla is run,
> most things work until mozilla trys to resolve one of the new  
> format function names or if it trys to call a vtable function.
> Since the vtable offset changed and/or the symbol is probably not
> getting resolved correcty, a bad address is loaded by the assembler
> code.  Depending on where the address points to, mozilla may run
> and just do nothing or it spontaneously crashes - in my experience
> anyway.  The patch adds logic to the assembler code code to
> recognize these differences when mozilla is compiled with GCC 3.x.
> 

Okay, good info.  I should probably try compiling with gcc-2.96 and
see how that binary performs.  

