I can confirm this. It happened to me when I used the 7.2
disk to do an upgrade but the upgrade did NOT include the
kernel. The old 'aboot.conf' is moved aside to 'aboot.conf.rpmsave'
and a new empty 'aboot.conf' is created
and presumably because a new kernel is not installed
the new 'aboot.conf' is not updated resulting in a valid
system that is very hard to boot. It is a bit unusual
to upgrade and exclude the kernel but there are some
valid resaons for doing this so I think it ought to work.

