On Wed, Mar 19, 2003 at 09:52:48AM -0800, Stephen Lewis wrote:
> 
> I can confirm this. It happened to me when I used the 7.2
> disk to do an upgrade but the upgrade did NOT include the
> kernel. The old 'aboot.conf' is moved aside to 'aboot.conf.rpmsave'
> and a new empty 'aboot.conf' is created

That most likely means that in '%files' section of a spec file
for 'aboot' that file is marked as '%config' instead of
'%config(noreplace)'.  Just guessing...  The later would make
'aboot.conf.rpmnew' but is something would later edit 'aboot.conf'
then there would be no backup unless editing script would make one.

> the new 'aboot.conf' is not updated resulting in a valid
> system that is very hard to boot.

What the world is coming to. :-)  Not so long time ago most
users were not even aware that something like 'aboot.conf'
even may exist and required long explanations.  I booted many
machines with no 'aboot.conf' at all.  It is tad inconvenient,
as you have to type the whole boot command explicitely, but
"very hard"?  Hardly. :-)

I am quite sure that among those who use milo as a boot loader
only a tiny minority heard that they can have 'milo.conf'
(more precisely, IIRC, either /etc/milo.conf or /milo.cnf where
paths are, obviously enough, relative to a top of your boot partition
which does not have to be the same as what will end up on /).

  Michal




