Greetings everyone,

Unfortunately I have still not succeeded installing an operating system on
my alpha, and I would appreciate some further advice.

The story so far...
Debian Woody crashes on a virtual memory request during installation whether
I boot from CD-ROM or floppy -it never gets to the stage of not recognising
the drives attached to the DAC960.

Red Hat 7.2 recognises the DAC960 but fails to recognise the drives,
apparently this is because it looks for version 4.73 firmware whereas the
most recent Digital firmware for this legacy card is 4.7.

There is a kernel patch from Richard Muratti which I am told will enable
RH7.2 to see the drives, however I failed to build a patched kernel on my
intel linux box, which reported hunks not applied.

There are more recent versions of the DAC960 firmware available on the Mylex
site and it has been suggested to me that I could transport the controller
to an intel box to flash it up then put it back in the 7305.

I have also been warned that using this procedure could render the card
inoperable because it is an OEM rather than a "Mylex" card, or because of
possible incompatibilities with the Digital SRM firmware.

I have sent a copy of this email to Mylex support in case they have any
comments.

Has anyone else successfully (or unsuccessfully) flashed a Mylex DAC960-PU
that came in an OEM machine with Digital firmware to a later Mylex firmware
(above 4.7)?

In theory upgrading the DAC960 to 4.73 should cause Red Hat to install, but
I daren't take the risk that it might be a one way ticket and trash the
card.

NEXT I TRIED
I have therefore been trying to RH7.2install to another hard drive to then
recompile a Kernel that recognises the DAC960 drives.

It seems to be impossible to daisy-chain a hard drive with a CDROM on the
NCR integrated SCSI bus.

This is because one end of the cable is split and attached to both the PCI
Board and Processor Board, the other end has only 1 female connector.

I have searched the internet for a double male internal 50 pin SCSI
connector (that would be needed to connect onto an additional cable), but
such a connector does not appear to exist.

I perservered with the cabling problems until I connected an Advansys PCI
SCSI host adaper with a Seagate drive attached.

The SRM reports that a SCSI controller card is present in  PCI slot 4 but it
does not detect the drive.

Neither does the Red Hat 7.2 installation programme, which doesn't see the
controller card either, and gives the same error message as it does with the
DAC960:-

-"no devices available on which to create filesystems".

If I type alphabios the ADVANSYS SCSI BIOS initializes and the SCSI card and
Seagate drive are both recognised by alphabios.

