Quoting Peter Watkinson <peter.watkinson1@ntlworld.com>:
> Hi there,
> I see on the Inquirer site it says that the AMD Opteron processor will
> be released in April, this is the server version.

April 22nd.

> Does anyone know how much these processors, motherboards, systems are
> likely to cost?

Not cheap.  AMD is releasing only the Opteron because quantities are still not
enough to fulfill consumer demand, and the speeds are still limited to 1.4, 1.6
and 1.8GHz at product launch.

The complementary Athlon 64 will be released in the fall as speeds exceed 2GHz
and quantities increase as fabrication technologies improve.

Technically, the two products differ as follows:

  Product================       Cache====  Integrated Controllers===
  Name       Codename      MP   L1   L2    Memory       Interconnect
  ---------- ------------  ---  ---- ----  ------------ ------------
  Opteron    Sledgehammer  2-8   128 1024  (2) 2.7GBps* (3) 6.4GBps
  Athlon 64  Clawhammer    no    128  ??*  (1) 2.7GBps* (1) 6.4GBps

Simply put, the Opteron has three (3) HyperTransport interconnects to facilitate
interconnecting to up to 8 processors (as well as PCI-X and other peripherial
tunnels), where the uniprocessor Athlon 64 has only one (for just PCI-X and
other peripherials).  The Opteron also has dual DDR333 memory controllers,
whereas the Athlon has only a single -- although AMD may switch to using DDR400
or DDR2 in a forthcoming revision.

The L2 cache on the Opteron is 1MB, whereas the Athlon 64 has not yet been
officially announced, and there might be different sizes -- from 256KB to 1MB.

