Quoting Peter Watkinson <peter.watkinson1@ntlworld.com>:
> Who cares if there is no Windows x86-64 for Opteron.

That's what EPIC and idSoftware and saying, as well as many other software
development houses who need >4GB, but don't like choosing between expensive
64-bit Itanium or EMS-equivalent 36-bit Xeon memory addressing.

> If there is the same amount of support for x86-64 Linux as there is
> 32bit x86 with aps like Open Office, Flash and Java in the browser
> etc then things could be pretty good.

GCC 3.2 optimizes for both Athlon and x86-64, and SuSE has had a working kernel
for over a year.  Heck, even AMD's x86-64 "Hammer" simulator was developed and
hosted on Linux.

> BTW on my other email account I got an email it seems from Redhat
> mentioning RH9.0 was that a fake?

Nope it's real, but not unexpected.  Red Hat is switching to a new "dot-less"
model for its "consumer" (aka "redistributable") versions.

I briefly covered that in this post on my local LUG:  
http://www.matrixlist.com/pipermail/leaplist/2003-March/029108.html  

In a nutshell, Red Hat is moving away from the 24-36 month, several revision
release cycle, to a split 60-month "Enterprise" and 12-month "consumer" product
line.  The trust QA and integration testing of the "consumer" version will
remain unchanged though, as the "Enterprise" versions are derrived from it.



