Quoting Peter Watkinson <peter.watkinson1@ntlworld.com>:
> Will it be possible to buy cpus, motherboards on their own?

Eventually.  But with quantities tight to start, OEMs and integrators will get
first access.

> Any idea how much these will cost?
> maybe roughly $500US for a cpu? Higher lower if you can't say any more
> :-)

$500+ is a safe bet.  Again, the first round of Opterons are really only for
those who need it that will pay for it.  So I don't blame AMD for making what
they can on it.

With the speeds only being 1.4-1.8GHz at release, dual-Athlon MP2600+ might not
be much slower at many applications than dual-Opteron (unless you really need
the added I/O and memory throughput).

Most will wait until Q3 when AMD starts producing 2GHz+ units, as well as the
release of the more consumer, uniprocessor Athlon 64.

> Any idea how it performs against say ev7? higher lower?

That's rather broad.  What is your application?

Again, the early Opterons won't offer much 32-bit performance beyond what you
get out of Athlon MP2400-2600+.  So unless you need more than 4GB of RAM, you're
not going to get much.

As far as I/O-wise, Opteron leverages AMD - API Networks' new and innovative
HyperTransport interconnect, which seems to be based on the engineering
expertise of former Digital developers who invented virtually every major
commodity system interconnect in Alphas and x86s we use today.  So that's different.

Hypertransport leapfrogs Intel in a game where everyone thought Intel was the
only player with its experimental serial I/O interconnect.  So much so that
everyone from Apple to Cray is licensing it.

