On Wed, 2003-03-26 at 02:32, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> Sorry, but I think you have fallen into the "Intel clockspeed trap".

Huh???  Not if I'm comparing _AMD_ to _AMD_!

Understand I'm comparing:

                 Top Clock   L1   L2   IReg    FPU-80   Memory Channels
                 ---------  ---- ----  ------  -------  ---------------
  Athlon MP "TB"  2.133GHz   128  256  32-bit  3-issue  (1) 2.1GBps
  Opteron         1.800GHz   128 1024  64-bit  3-issue  (2) 2.7GBps

[ NOTE:  Athlon MP "TB" 2.133GHz is the MP2600+ "Thunderbird-B,"
available today for under $300 retail. ]

> I think you will find on certain types of operations, especially those that 
> gain by fast memory

Agreed, which brings back _memory_ and _I/O_ throughput which I _did_
say _would_ make "all the difference."  Especially when talking
dual-processor -- and beyond, which only Opteron does.

I'm sure the 4x L2 cache improves performance some 20% as well.

> and floating point,

Now hold on there!

The 80-bit FPU in the Opteron does _not_ change from its current 3-issue
incarnation in the 32-bit Athlon.

Yes, the number of 128-bit registers (also used for 80-bit FPU) are
doubled, and some register renaming _could_ improve performance, along
with other optimizations, but not more than 10-20%.

> you will be pleasantly surprised to  find out just how fast a
> "only 1.8GHz" Opteron performs.

Internal AMD benchmarks labelled it an "3400+".

So, again, is it worth the cost at this point?

Or is it better to wait for the 2nd-gen Opteron/Athlon64 to come out in
