With regards to your message at 12:44 AM 3/26/03, Bryan J. Smith. Where you 
stated:
>On Wed, 2003-03-26 at 02:32, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> > Sorry, but I think you have fallen into the "Intel clockspeed trap".
>
>Huh???  Not if I'm comparing _AMD_ to _AMD_!
Yes, even there.
As somebody already mentioned, their Alpha at 833MHz still does some things 
considerably faster than CPUs (AMD *or* Intel) at 2Ghz.
The Opteron should easily beat an Athlon at similar clock speeds.

>Understand I'm comparing:
>
>                  Top Clock   L1   L2   IReg    FPU-80   Memory Channels
>                  ---------  ---- ----  ------  -------  ---------------
>   Athlon MP "TB"  2.133GHz   128  256  32-bit  3-issue  (1) 2.1GBps
>   Opteron         1.800GHz   128 1024  64-bit  3-issue  (2) 2.7GBps
>
>[ NOTE:  Athlon MP "TB" 2.133GHz is the MP2600+ "Thunderbird-B,"
>available today for under $300 retail. ]
I agree that $ for flops it might be different.
As you say, the Athlon is "almost free"

> > I think you will find on certain types of operations, especially those 
> that
> > gain by fast memory
>
>Agreed, which brings back _memory_ and _I/O_ throughput which I _did_
>say _would_ make "all the difference."  Especially when talking
>dual-processor -- and beyond, which only Opteron does.
Fair enough and true.

>I'm sure the 4x L2 cache improves performance some 20% as well.
>
> > and floating point,
>
>Now hold on there!
>
>The 80-bit FPU in the Opteron does _not_ change from its current 3-issue
>incarnation in the 32-bit Athlon.
True, but IF you are using 674 bit code, it IS 64 bits wide.

>Yes, the number of 128-bit registers (also used for 80-bit FPU) are
>doubled, and some register renaming _could_ improve performance, along
>with other optimizations, but not more than 10-20%.
Well, the Digital and Intel compilers regularly produce improvements like 
that on code performance, so I would not be totally shocked if the 
improvements in the CPU technology have a good effect.
Having said that, it will remain to be seen if compilers need to be 
improved to make that happen.

> > you will be pleasantly surprised to  find out just how fast a
> > "only 1.8GHz" Opteron performs.
>
>Internal AMD benchmarks labelled it an "3400+".
Right, and I think that is about correct, as far as a comparison on 32 bit 
ops is concerned.

>So, again, is it worth the cost at this point?
>
>Or is it better to wait for the 2nd-gen Opteron/Athlon64 to come out in
>the fall with a "4000+" rating against Pentium 4?


It is ALWAYS better to wait for new technology.
Unless, of course, you need the processing power now, in which case you 
have to decide what will best fit the need.
And, of course, what your strategy will be over the next couple of years.


