Quoting Maurice Hilarius <maurice@harddata.com>:
> As somebody already mentioned, their Alpha at 833MHz still does some
> things considerably faster than CPUs (AMD *or* Intel) at 2Ghz.

Of course.  Alpha was the best RISC design ever.  Anal to the core, Digital
engineers took RISC and made it better by _never_ putting an instruction in that
would take more than a few cycles.

> The Opteron should easily beat an Athlon at similar clock speeds.

In just raw CPU operations, 25-40% is expected from what I've read.  In
memory-I/O intensive operations, 2x or greater is possible.

> I agree that $ for flops it might be different.
> As you say, the Athlon is "almost free"

Okay, here's my view.

Unless you need 4+ way, or serious memory-I/O throughput, then *I* feel Athlon
MP is of a better value right now.  But that _will_ change when the 2GHz+
Opterons (and Athlon64s) come out later in the year for less money.

If I was an engineering or research company considering Opteron, I'd spend $1.5K
getting a solid Athlon MP platform right now, then $3-4K on a dual-2.2GHz
(possibly higher?) Opteron later in the year, _instead_ of spending $5K on a
dual-1.8GHz Opteron now.

> Fair enough and true.

Okay, cool.

> True, but IF you are using 674 bit code, it IS 64 bits wide.

It doesn't matter if your IReg is 32-bit or 64-bit, the x87 is 80-bit in _all_
AMD products.  And the 3-issue FPU in the Opteron remains largely _unchanged_
from the Athlon, with exception of a few more registers and some minor
optimizations.

> Well, the Digital and Intel compilers regularly produce improvements
> like that on code performance, so I would not be totally shocked if the 
> improvements in the CPU technology have a good effect.
> Having said that, it will remain to be seen if compilers need to be 
> improved to make that happen.

GCC 3.2 does a lot from what I've seen.

> Right, and I think that is about correct, as far as a comparison on 32
> bit ops is concerned.

Er, it's all debatable right now.

Again, my point is the fact that 32-bit v. 64-bit IReg doesn't mean _squat_ when
it comes to FPU design and performance performance.

> It is ALWAYS better to wait for new technology.

But even more so when a technology is _new_.

The first gen is _always_ for "early adopters."  The second gen is for the
masses.  Especially when second gen is _only_ 4 months away!

> Unless, of course, you need the processing power now, in which case you
> have to decide what will best fit the need.
> And, of course, what your strategy will be over the next couple of
> years.

My point is that with 2nd gen Opteron, we're only talking about 4 months (and
not years)!  Again, I'd wait.


