>>>>> "Bryan" == Bryan J Smith <b.j.smith@ieee.org> writes:

cloos> At least for posix systems ... The abi for longmode
cloos> specifies -fpmath=sse.

Bryan> And that will give you double-precision math too?

Yes.  But only at 64-bit precision, not 80-bit rounded to 64.  This
does mean that rounding will be identical to other ieee 754 platforms,
such as (since we are on this list) alpha.  Ie, results of fp
algorithms will be the same on hammer as on the other, non x86-32,
platforms.

cloos> That should provide a significant boost compared to -fpmath=387.
cloos> Probably not a *hugh* boost, but a statisticly significant one.

Bryan> At how much of a significant digit cost?  From my
Bryan> understanding, SSE operations are rather "lossy" math.

The results should be identical to any other 754 cpu.

Bryan> E.g., in the new "long" mode, is "-fpmath=sse" more than just
Bryan> SSE instructions, and a whole new way of using/scheduling in
Bryan> the FPU?

The extra boost would be due to the register based design, rather than
the stack based design used by the 387.  Combined with 16 xmm
registers and the possibility to vectorize the algorithms -- I don't
know whether gcc will automatically do that yet, but compilers
certainly are free to -- compilers should be able to produce faster
code that can be done on the 387.

I should note this is all based on what I've read; both on lists and
in the x86-64 architecture manuals (cf www.x86-64.org for a pointer to
the pdf version and the form to request a free hardcopy edition.)

-JimC




