On Thu, Mar 27, 2003 at 10:01:34AM -0500, George France wrote:
> The installer (fdisk) has always had problems with DOS labeled disks.  Please 
> boot the install CD into rescue mode, run fdisk, type 'b'

With a version of 'fdisk' which you have on CDs and on anaconda images
you will get here a nice "Segmentation violation" or other bomb of that
sort.  You need to use 'dd' from /dev/zero to erase an old FAT table
first ('count=1', or 'count=2' if you prefer a belt and suspenders, will
be just fine).

Few years ago I supplied a repeatable test case which does not require
messing with your hard disks and some proposed patches both to Red Hat
and to Andries Brouwer who maintains fdisk.  All of that was carefully
ignored.

> We do have a specally patched version of fdisk that handles this issue 
> correctly that will be on the new ISO images that are planned for release 
> later in April.

Well, so maybe more users will see an fdisk which is not spooked by
an old partitioning information.  The other issue is that DiskDruid
keeps, correctly, a partitioning type which is already present and, this
time incorrectly, does not provide any means to force a change.

None of the above is really critical, in that sense that it is simple
to work around,  but a big PITA if you are a newbie.

  Michal




