On Thu, Mar 27, 2003 at 08:46:00AM -0500, Iain Barker wrote:
> 
> Also, if it does then what if any performance difference is noticed?

Regardless of other issues big gains with 'ccc' are for programs which
use heavily a floating point (with a big portion of these gains coming
from using different libraries which you can do with 'gcc' as well but
in a "normal" setup not by default but when you will ask explicitely).

Your kernel is not using a floting point at all and in such situations
there were examples of programs where 'gcc' generated code was actually
faster than that compiled with 'ccc'.  This does not mean that this
would be the case here, if you would accomplish such feat, but if your
machine spends a significant portion of resources inside of a kernel
then you have much more serious problems.

   Michal




